{"name":"Blue Iris","hex":"#6264a6","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"iris","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Iris, common in the northern hemisphere, and generally having attractive blooms (See  Iris (plant) on  Wikipedia.Wikipedia ).","The contractile membrane perforated by the pupil, which adjusts to control the amount of light reaching the retina, and which forms the colored portion of the eye (See  Iris (anatomy) on  Wikipedia.Wikipedia )."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open or close in the manner of an iris.","To cause (something) to shine with the colours of the rainbow; to make iridescent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iris"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iris spuria","blocks":[{"text":"Iris spuria, or blue flag, is a species of the genus Iris, part of the subgenus Limniris and the series Spuriae. It is a rhizomatous perennial plant, from Europe, Asia and Africa. It has purple or lilac flowers, and slender, elongated leaves. It is widely cultivated as an ornamental plant in temperate regions and hybridized for use in the garden. It has several subspecies; Iris spuria subsp. carthaliniae B.Mathew, Iris spuria subsp. demetrii B.Mathew, Iris spuria subsp. maritima (Dykes) P.Fourn. and Iris spuria subsp. musulmanica (Fomin) Takht. It used to have 3 other subspecies, which have now been re-classified as separate species; Iris spuria subsp. halophila, Iris spuria ssp. sogdiana and Iris spuria subsp. notha .\nIt has many common names including 'blue iris', 'spurious iris' and 'bastard iris'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Iris_spuria.jpg/330px-Iris_spuria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_spuria","wikidata":"Q163083"},{"title":"Iris (anatomy)","blocks":[{"text":"The iris is a thin, ring-shaped structure in the eye in most mammals and birds that is responsible for controlling the diameter and size of the pupil, and thus the amount of light reaching the retina. In optical terms, the pupil is the eye's aperture, while the iris is the diaphragm. Eye color is defined by the iris."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Human_eye_close_up%2C_anterior_view.jpg/330px-Human_eye_close_up%2C_anterior_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(anatomy)","wikidata":"Q178748"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"},{"title":"Iris (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Iris is an ambiguous color term, usually referring to shades ranging from blue-violet to violet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Unidentified_Iris_Chanticleer_Purple_2868px.jpg/330px-Unidentified_Iris_Chanticleer_Purple_2868px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(color)","wikidata":"Q6070109"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Iris_spuria_Sturm62.jpg/500px-Iris_spuria_Sturm62.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Iris_spuria_Sturm62.jpg/500px-Iris_spuria_Sturm62.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Iris spuria illustration in:Jakob Sturm: \"Deutschlands Flora in Abbildungen\" Stuttgart (1796)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIris_spuria_Sturm62.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Iris_spuria_%28Curtis%29.jpg/500px-Iris_spuria_%28Curtis%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Iris_spuria_%28Curtis%29.jpg/500px-Iris_spuria_%28Curtis%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration from William Curtis's The Botanical Magazine (V. 2), in 1790.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIris_spuria_(Curtis).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg/500px-Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg/1280px-Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg/500px-Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The iris (brown coloured portion of the eye) controls the size of the pupil by contracting the sphincter pupillae and dilator pupillae muscles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APupillary_light_reflex.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Iris_regions.jpg/500px-Iris_regions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Iris_regions.jpg/1280px-Iris_regions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Iris_regions.jpg/500px-Iris_regions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIris_regions.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Auge_Iris.jpg/500px-Auge_Iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Auge_Iris.jpg/1280px-Auge_Iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Auge_Iris.jpg/500px-Auge_Iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Light micrograph of the iris near to the pupil. M. sph. sphincter muscle, L lens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAuge_Iris.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif/500px-Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif/500px-Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Constriction of the pupil (miosis) observed by laser Doppler imaging reveals radial vessels of the iris.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADoppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache's_myosis.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif/500px-Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif/500px-Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A human eye demonstrating its owner's rare ability to voluntarily dilate and constrict his pupil on command, via voluntary control of his iris muscles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVoluntary_pupil_dilation.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png/500px-SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png/500px-SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anterior chamber cross-section imaged by an SD-OCT.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_(with_viewfinder).png"}],"sounds":{"blue":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/En-uk-blue.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/8f/En-uk-blue.ogg/En-uk-blue.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audi